              "prompt": "Large language models are based on the transformer architecture. What is the attention formula in transformers? Explain the attention operation in relation to $Q$, $K$, and $V$.",
              "promptHtml": "<p>Large language models are based on the transformer architecture. What is the attention formula in transformers? Explain the attention operation in relation to <span class=\"inline-math\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></math></span>, and <span class=\"inline-math\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p>",
              "answer": "Scaled dot-product attention is \\operatorname{Attention}(Q,K,V)=\\operatorname{softmax}\\left(\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right)V. Queries $Q$ represent what each position is looking for, keys $K$ represent what each position offers for matching, and values $V$ are the information that gets mixed. Dot products score query-key compatibility, softmax turns scores into attention weights, and multiplying by $V$ returns a weighted combination of value vectors.",
              "answerHtml": "<p>Scaled dot-product attention is</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Display formula\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>Attention</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>softmax</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Queries <span class=\"inline-math\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math></span> represent what each position is looking for, keys <span class=\"inline-math\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></math></span> represent what each position offers for matching, and values <span class=\"inline-math\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math></span> are the information that gets mixed. Dot products score query-key compatibility, softmax turns scores into attention weights, and multiplying by <span class=\"inline-math\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math></span> returns a weighted combination of value vectors.</p>",
              "explanation": "Mention the $\\sqrt{d_k}$ scaling and the role of the softmax weights.",
