        "front": "Example 3d (Score-based Models): Define the score function. What is the benefit of using it?",
        "answer": "The score is s(x)=\\nabla_x\\log p(x). It points toward locally higher density and removes constants such as $\\log Z_\\theta$ because gradients with respect to $x$ of constants are zero.",
        "answerHtml": "<p>The score is</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Display formula\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02207;</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>It points toward locally higher density and removes constants such as <span class=\"inline-math\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> because gradients with respect to <span class=\"inline-math\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span> of constants are zero.</p>",
        "explanation": "The benefit is both normalization cancellation and a sampling direction.",
