        "front": "Example 4f (LLMs): The formulation of perplexity is \\operatorname{Perplexity}_\\theta(X)=\\exp\\left[-\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\log P_\\theta(x_i\\mid x_1,\\ldots,x_{i-1})\\right]. What does perplexity measure? Is higher better or lower better? Explain your reasoning.",
        "answer": "Perplexity is the exponentiated average negative log-likelihood per token. It measures how surprised the model is by the sequence, or roughly the effective branching factor. Lower perplexity is better because it means the model assigns higher probability to the observed tokens.",
        "answerHtml": "<p>Perplexity is the exponentiated average negative log-likelihood per token. It measures how surprised the model is by the sequence, or roughly the effective branching factor. Lower perplexity is better because it means the model assigns higher probability to the observed tokens.</p>",
        "explanation": "Connect the sign: lower negative log-likelihood means lower perplexity and better predictive fit.",
