        "lectureTitle": "Example exam · Score-based Models",
        "conceptIds": [
          "w5-score-concept-score-matching"
        ],
        "prompt": "The score matching loss is L(x)=\\frac12\\mathbb E_{x\\sim p(x)}[s_\\theta(x)^2]+\\mathbb E_{x\\sim p(x)}[\\nabla_x s_\\theta(x)]. Give an interpretation of the two terms.",
        "promptHtml": "<p>The score matching loss is</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Display formula\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>&#x0007E;</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>&#x0007E;</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mo>&#x02207;</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Give an interpretation of the two terms.</p>",
        "answer": "The squared-score term penalizes unnecessarily large score magnitudes. The derivative/divergence term shapes the vector field so it matches how probability mass changes around the data distribution. Together they are the integration-by-parts form of matching the model score to the data score without knowing the data score directly.",
        "answerHtml": "<p>The squared-score term penalizes unnecessarily large score magnitudes. The derivative/divergence term shapes the vector field so it matches how probability mass changes around the data distribution. Together they are the integration-by-parts form of matching the model score to the data score without knowing the data score directly.</p>",
        "explanation": "For multidimensional data, use divergence or trace-of-Jacobian language.",
