        "prompt": "In practice, we have access to only finite data points for training. What is the problem with having a finite dataset $D$?",
        "promptHtml": "<p>In practice, we have access to only finite data points for training. What is the problem with having a finite dataset <span class=\"inline-math\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span>?</p>",
        "answer": "The empirical distribution is a collection of discrete points / delta masses. Its score is not a useful smooth vector field in the surrounding space, so it gives poor guidance between data points and in low-density regions.",
        "answerHtml": "<p>The empirical distribution is a collection of discrete points / delta masses. Its score is not a useful smooth vector field in the surrounding space, so it gives poor guidance between data points and in low-density regions.</p>",
