        "prompt": "For sampling, we follow the learned scores. However, instead of directly following the score, we further add noise in each step. Why?",
        "promptHtml": "<p>For sampling, we follow the learned scores. However, instead of directly following the score, we further add noise in each step. Why?</p>",
        "answer": "Adding noise makes the process a sampler rather than pure gradient ascent to a mode. It helps exploration, avoids collapsing to local maxima, and gives Langevin dynamics the desired stochastic behavior for sampling from the target distribution.",
        "answerHtml": "<p>Adding noise makes the process a sampler rather than pure gradient ascent to a mode. It helps exploration, avoids collapsing to local maxima, and gives Langevin dynamics the desired stochastic behavior for sampling from the target distribution.</p>",
        "explanation": "The score term moves toward high density; the noise term maintains stochastic exploration.",
